<h2>The N+1 Rule, and Why It’s Not Arbitrary</h2>
<p>The standard guidance from feline behaviorists and organizations including the ASPCA and AAHA is <strong>one litter box per cat, plus one extra</strong> — one cat needs two boxes, three cats need four. This isn’t about volume of waste; a single cat physically doesn’t need four boxes’ worth of capacity. It exists because of the guarding behavior described above: with only as many boxes as cats, a confident cat can control access to all of them. Extra boxes in separate locations make guarding logistically difficult, which is the actual goal.</p>
<h3>Where to Put Them</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Spread across multiple locations</strong>, not clustered in one utility room — clustering defeats the purpose of having extra boxes at all</li>
<li><strong>Quiet, low-traffic spots</strong> — a hallway everyone walks through all day is stressful to eliminate in</li>
<li><strong>Away from food and water</strong> — cats instinctively avoid toileting near where they eat</li>
<li><strong>One per floor level</strong> for households with stairs, especially with a senior or arthritic cat who may simply stop climbing rather than complain about it</li>
<li><strong>Never in a tight, cornered dead-end</strong> — cats want a visible escape route while vulnerable</li>
</ul>
<h2>Depth, Cleaning Frequency, and Replacement</h2>
<p>Most cats prefer <strong>2–4 inches</strong> of litter — enough to dig and bury properly without being so deep that kittens or arthritic seniors struggle to walk through it.</p>
